WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.301 --> 00:00:04.600
In about half an hour, I heard rustling in the thicket behind me.

2
00:00:04.601 --> 00:00:09.378
Bill staggered out of the bushes with the kid about eight feet behind him.

3
00:00:09.379 --> 00:00:14.834
The boy was stepping softly like a cat and had a broad grin on his face.

4
00:00:14.836 --> 00:00:19.913
Bill stopped, took off his hat, and wiped his face with a red handkerchief.

5
00:00:19.914 --> 00:00:30.139
"Sam," Bill said, "you're going to think I betrayed you, but I couldn't help it. The boy is gone. I've sent him home. Everything is off.

6
00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:38.687
Old legends tell of men," Bill went on, "who suffered hardship and even death rather than give up in the face of adversity.

7
00:00:38.688 --> 00:00:42.544
But none of them was ever tortured as much as I've been."

8
00:00:42.545 --> 00:00:45.218
"What's the trouble?" I asked him.

9
00:00:45.219 --> 00:00:55.478
"He rode me," Bill said, "the ninety miles to the fort, not an inch less. Then when we rescued the settlers, I was given horse feed.

10
00:00:55.479 --> 00:00:59.462
Need I tell you that sand is not a substitute for oats?

11
00:00:59.463 --> 00:01:09.212
After that, I had to explain to him why there is nothing in a hole, how a road can run both ways, and what makes the grass green.

12
00:01:09.213 --> 00:01:17.520
I tell you, Sam, a man can only stand so much. I grabbed him by the collar and dragged him down the mountain.

13
00:01:17.521 --> 00:01:25.961
He kicked my legs black and blue all the way. Look! I've got three bites on my hand that should be seen by a doctor.

14
00:01:25.962 --> 00:01:33.292
"So he's gone," Bill continued. "Gone home. I showed him the road to Summit and shoved him toward it.

15
00:01:33.293 --> 00:01:41.022
I'm sorry that we're going to lose the ransom, but it was either that or Bill Driscoll ends up a crazy man."

16
00:01:41.023 --> 00:01:47.322
Bill was out of breath, but there was a look of overwhelming peace and growing contentment on his face.

17
00:01:47.323 --> 00:01:52.465
"Bill," I said, "there isn't any heart disease in your family, is there?"

18
00:01:52.466 --> 00:01:59.040
"No," he said, "nothing unusual except malaria and a few accidents. Why?"

19
00:01:59.041 --> 00:02:03.755
"Then turn around," I said, "and have a look behind you."

20
00:02:03.756 --> 00:02:08.477
Bill turned, saw the boy, and his face went white.

21
00:02:08.478 --> 00:02:15.074
He plopped down in the dirt and began to pluck at the grass and little sticks on the ground for no reason at all.

22
00:02:15.075 --> 00:02:17.803
I was afraid he'd lost his mind.

23
00:02:17.804 --> 00:02:23.240
And then I said, "My plan is to get this job over with immediately.

24
00:02:23.241 --> 00:02:28.842
We'll get the ransom and be on the road with the money by midnight if old Dorset agrees."

25
00:02:28.843 --> 00:02:33.754
Bill took a deep breath and nodded. He gave Red Chief a weak sort of smile.

26
00:02:33.755 --> 00:02:42.787
"As soon as I feel a little better, kid, I'll play General Custer and you can be Sitting Bull at the Battle of the Little Bighorn. I promise."

27
00:02:42.788 --> 00:02:50.029
I had a great plan for collecting the ransom so I wouldn't be caught. How proud kidnappers everywhere would be!

28
00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:57.214
The tree under which Dorset's answer was to be left—was next to the road, with big open fields on all sides.

29
00:02:57.215 --> 00:03:07.034
Naturally, if anyone might be watching for the kidnapper to come for the note, they'd be able to see him a long way off crossing the fields or walking in the road.

30
00:03:07.035 --> 00:03:09.106
But they wouldn't see me!

31
00:03:09.107 --> 00:03:16.996
At half-past eight, I was up in that tree, as well hidden as a tree toad, waiting for the messenger to arrive.

32
00:03:16.997 --> 00:03:23.877
Exactly on time, a boy rode up on a bicycle and found the cardboard box at the foot of the fence post.

33
00:03:23.878 --> 00:03:27.707
He slipped a folded piece of paper into it and pedaled away.

34
00:03:27.708 --> 00:03:30.940
I waited an hour until I was sure I was safe.

35
00:03:30.941 --> 00:03:39.290
Then I slid down the tree, got the note, crept along the fence until I hit the woods, and was back at the cave in another half an hour.

36
00:03:39.291 --> 00:03:42.423
I opened the note near the lantern and read it to Bill.

37
00:03:42.424 --> 00:03:44.208
Two Desperate Men

38
00:03:44.209 --> 00:03:50.253
Gentlemen: I received your letter today regarding the ransom for the return of my son.

39
00:03:50.254 --> 00:03:55.865
I think your demands are a little high, so I'll make you a counter-offer that I believe you will accept.

40
00:03:55.866 --> 00:04:03.674
You bring Johnny home and pay me two hundred and fifty dollars in cash, and I agree to take him off your hands.

41
00:04:03.675 --> 00:04:09.299
You'd better come at night, because the neighbors believe he is lost and gone for good.

42
00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:13.927
I won't be responsible for what they would do to you for bringing that boy back.

43
00:04:13.928 --> 00:04:15.756
Very respectfully,

44
00:04:15.758 --> 00:04:17.938
EBENEZER DORSET

45
00:04:17.939 --> 00:04:21.611
"For crying out loud," I said, "of all the nerve—"

46
00:04:21.612 --> 00:04:28.381
But I glanced at Bill, and I stopped. He had the most pleading look in his eyes I ever saw.

47
00:04:28.382 --> 00:04:34.491
"Sam," he said, "what's two hundred and fifty dollars, after all? We got the money.

48
00:04:34.492 --> 00:04:38.466
One more night of this kid will send me to the insane asylum.

49
00:04:38.467 --> 00:04:44.447
Mr. Dorset is giving us a bargain. You're not going to let this chance go, are you?"

50
00:04:44.448 --> 00:04:51.749
"To tell you the truth, Bill," I said, "this tender little lamb of a boy has gotten on my nerves too.

51
00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:55.385
We'll take him home, pay the ransom, and make our getaway."

52
00:04:55.386 --> 00:04:57.557
We took him back that night.

53
00:04:57.558 --> 00:05:01.614
"I don't want to go home!" the kid said when he saw his house.

54
00:05:01.615 --> 00:05:06.597
"Oh, yes you do," I told him. "Your father bought a silver rifle for you."

55
00:05:06.598 --> 00:05:09.106
"And a pair of moccasins," Bill added.

56
00:05:09.107 --> 00:05:11.943
"He did?" Red Chief asked.

57
00:05:11.944 --> 00:05:17.165
"Yep." Bill nodded. "And we're all going bear hunting tomorrow. You'll see."

58
00:05:17.166 --> 00:05:20.887
It was just twelve o'clock when we knocked on Ebenezer's front door.

59
00:05:20.888 --> 00:05:30.046
Just when I should have been taking the fifteen hundred dollars from the box under the tree, Bill was counting out two hundred and fifty dollars into Dorset's hand.

60
00:05:30.047 --> 00:05:36.657
"You're leaving me here?" the kid said when he figured things out. "NOOO! NOOO!"

61
00:05:36.658 --> 00:05:42.411
He howled like a wounded coyote and grabbed Bill's leg. "NOOO!"

62
00:05:42.412 --> 00:05:47.088
His father peeled him away gradually, as Red Chief continued to kick and scream.

63
00:05:47.089 --> 00:05:50.050
"How long can you hold him?" asked Bill.

64
00:05:50.051 --> 00:05:57.156
"I'm not as strong as I used to be," said Old Dorset, "but I think I can promise you ten minutes."

65
00:05:57.157 --> 00:06:03.193
"That's enough," said Bill. "In ten minutes, I'll be crossing the Canadian border."

66
00:06:03.194 --> 00:06:05.724
Bill took off running through the darkness.

67
00:06:05.725 --> 00:06:13.727
And even though he was very fat and I'm a very good runner, he was a good mile and a half out of Summit before I could catch up with him.

